      heading: "Water Heater Services FAQs",
      items: [
        {
          q: "How often should a water heater be serviced?",
          preview: "Once a year is a good rule for most homes.",
          full: [
            "Once a year is a good rule for most homes. If you have hard water, ",
            { tag: "a", attrs: { href: "/water-heater-maintenance" }, kids: ["a flush every six months"] },
            " helps keep mineral buildup from wearing the tank down. A yearly visit also lets a technician test the relief valve, which keeps pressure in the tank at a safe level.",
          ],
        },
        {
          q: "Should I repair or replace my water heater?",
          preview: "It depends on the unit’s age, what’s wrong and what the repair costs compared to a new unit.",
          full: "It depends on the unit’s age, what’s wrong and what the repair costs compared to a new unit. A repair usually makes sense for a unit under 8 years old with one failed part. If the tank is leaking, the unit is more than 10 to 12 years old or the repair costs close to half the price of a new one, replacement usually makes more sense. We show you both options before you decide.",
        },
        {
          q: "How long do water heaters last?",
          preview: "Most tank water heaters last about 8 to 12 years.",
          full: "Most tank water heaters last about 8 to 12 years. Tankless units can last 20 years or more with regular descaling. Hard water and skipped flushes shorten both, because minerals build up and wear out the tank and parts faster.",
        },
        {
          q: "What is the most common water heater problem?",
          preview: "Mineral buildup, also called sediment.",
          full: "Mineral buildup, also called sediment. Minerals in the water settle at the bottom of the tank over time. That buildup causes popping or rumbling sounds, makes hot water run out faster and can lead to leaks as it wears the tank down. A yearly flush is the simplest way to prevent it.",
        },
        {
          q: "How much does it cost to service a water heater?",
          preview: "It depends on the type of water heater, its age, what’s wrong and which parts it needs.",
          full: "It depends on the type of water heater, its age, what’s wrong and which parts it needs. Gas, electric and tankless units all need different work. Our technician finds the problem first and gives you a flat rate before any work starts. Give us a call and we’ll walk you through it.",
        },
        {
          q: "Who should I call to service my water heater?",
          preview: "Call a plumber, not a handyman.",
          full: "Call a plumber, not a handyman. Water heater work often means handling gas lines, exhaust venting and high-voltage wiring, and it has to meet local code. A mistake can lead to a gas leak or a carbon monoxide problem. Our technicians are trained on gas, electric and tankless units, so give us a call.",
        },
      ],
